<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marino Sanudo Torsello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1270–1343</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marino Sanudo Torsello was a Venetian statesman and geographer who travelled widely in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. His Secreta fidelium crucis proposed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> with an economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and his history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> describes the Frankish states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, including the French-speaking court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marino-sanudo-torsello.jpg" alt="Marino Sanudo Torsello" width="320" height="292" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marino Sanuto the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodleian_Library_MS._Tanner_190,_fol._4r,_initial.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
